<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uta von Ballenstedt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–<a href="/years/1046">1046</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Uta of Naumburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uta von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noblewoman, the wife of Margrave Ekkehard II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. Her statue by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-master">Naumburg Master</a> in the west choir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-cathedral">Naumburg Cathedral</a>, carved about two centuries after her death, became one of the most celebrated figures of medieval German art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uta-von-ballenstedt.jpg" alt="Uta von Ballenstedt" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linsengericht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naumburg-Uta.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of her descent. She belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a> and was probably the sister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Esico of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a>, the forefather of the Ascanian dynasty. Count Adalbert of Ballenstedt and Hidda, a daughter of the Lusatian margrave Odo I, are commonly taken to be their parents, though no contemporary source names them. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> chronicles of the 13th century, her father married her around 1026 to the younger brother of Margrave Herman I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, presumably for political reasons, to further the rise of her family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her husband, a loyal supporter of the Salian king Henry III, became Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and in 1038, on the death of his elder brother, also took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, making Uta margravine. The marriage remained childless, so the Ekkeharding dynasty died out with him. After her death he gave large parts of her dowry to the convent of Gernrode in her home country, where her sister Hazecha had been made abbess by Henry III in 1044. The rest of her estates passed to Empress Agnes of Poitou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As one of the donors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-cathedral">Naumburg Cathedral</a> she was given a painted statue in the 13th century, one of twelve life-sized founders standing in a semicircle in the Early Gothic west choir, a group of nobles below the rank of king that is unique in art history. From the early 20th century her image, with its upturned collar, became an icon of supposedly genuine German character. The Nazi regime used it as an Aryan model, in Fritz Hippler's film The Eternal Jew and in wartime propaganda. The statue may have inspired the Evil Queen in Disney's 1937 film Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uta von Ballenstedt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Naumburg Cathedral's west choir, made around 1250 by the anonymous Naumburg Master, holds twelve statues of the cathedral's founders, including the margravine <b>Uta</b>, among the finest works of Gothic sculpture.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.72,11.24&amp;year=1029" aria-label="Ballenstedt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Uta von Ballenstedt"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 5655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 5655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.72,11.24&amp;year=1029">What was here in 1029? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
